    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Menace to Sobriety is Ugly Kid Joe’s second full-length album, originally released in 1995; it presents a noticeably heavier, darker hard‑rock sound that pushed the band away from the more overtly humorous, funk‑tinged blend of their early work toward a muscular, riff‑driven approach. The record's style emphasizes thicker guitar tones, aggressive rhythms and more serious lyrical themes while retaining occasional melodic hooks and the band’s wry sensibility, making it a stylistic bridge between the commercial rock of America’s Least Wanted and the even heavier directions the group explored on subsequent releases.\n\nIn the band’s discography Menace to Sobriety occupies a turning‑point role as their second studio LP and follow‑up to their 1992 breakthrough, marking Ugly Kid Joe’s move into darker, more hard‑edged territory; it was issued in 1995 on Mercury (and later associated reissues have appeared on DEKO/DEKO Entertainment). Critically the album received mixed-to-positive attention—some outlets (for example Kerrang!) praised it highly at the time—while commercially it did not match the multi‑platinum success of their debut, a situation the band attributed in part to limited label support in the U.S..",
